/* ---- the lanes of Tyre: the lane map ---- */
/* The island city, laid out in its own units and drawn 1.3 times as large (SC), so that its people stand about fourteen room pixels tall;
   q scales a length, X and Y a place, pts a line or an outline, box a rectangle. The plan is invented: the publications say that Tyre was
   an island city joined to the mainland by Alexander's causeway, a busy port known for its glass, its metalwork and its purple dye, and
   nothing of its lanes. It is not a grid: the lanes bend and fork, and none crosses another (CONTRIBUTING section 3). The harbor gate is
   at the left (the quay of tyre17 lies beyond it), the causeway's gate at the right; the sea lies round the edges.
   The way: the main street from the harbor gate to the market square; from the square two lanes go north, the one at the left to a
   court where the Jewish woman lives, the one at the right to the glassworkers' yard; from the yard a lane north to a fork, its left
   branch to a court before a house that gives no reason to knock, its right branch to the court before the house where the disciples
   meet. Dead ends lie off the main street and off the yard. */
